After introducing the explicit provisional-review marker, the focused review regression suite also passed: `bun test --timeout 30000 tests/integration/13-review-loop.test.ts` — 18 passed, 0 failed, 157 assertions. Missing required validation attestation still cannot become verified.
# Local package distribution

## Summary

Clean builds pack the runtime, dashboard and reviewer resources into a locally installable tarball. Tests install it in two real Git consumers and import the installed entry point and public modules, rather than certifying source imports as packaging evidence.

## Key behavior

- Version 0.2.0 targets OpenCode 2.0.29 and ships root `server.js`/`tui.js` entry points. Configure the package directory in `plugins`, not a compiled file in the v1 `plugin` list.
- `build` removes stale `dist` output; the manifest allowlists runtime resources and installation docs. Developer-local self-dependencies and runtime log canaries are regression checked.
- Fixture repositories have paths with spaces, relative tarball dependencies, distinct stores/locks and dashboard listeners. The installed process wrapper is tested for argv, readiness and tree cleanup.
- Real-server restart exposed a reentrant initialization deadlock. `src/plugin.ts` now defers recovery status events until initialization returns; the durable recovery summary is recorded synchronously. Disposal cancels pending notifications. Never await an SDK request into the host during plugin startup.
- `process` exposes the existing ownership API for approved command owners. It does not discover or approve commands.
- The deterministic installed demo drives setup, approved planning, real Git/TDD execution/integration and explicit dual review, and checks `verified` versus `incomplete-validation`. Its passing fixtures do not certify a complete product workflow or a live provider.
- CI runs all suites on macOS, Linux and native Windows without exclusions. Credentialed session smoke stays separately gated; production input uses ordinary chat replies bound to pending requests and production scheduling coordinates both reviews.

## Code references

- `src/plugin.ts` — non-blocking startup notifications, covered by the deferred-notification regression in issue 15.
- `package.json`, `scripts/clean-build.ts` — clean build, resource allowlist and exports.
- `tests/helpers/packaged-workflow.ts` — installed public setup-to-report fixture, real TDD/Git and timeout omission.
- `src/core/types.ts`, `src/execution/scheduler.ts` — optional `reviewPending` distinguishes provisional scheduler state from missing validation attestation.
- `src/review/coordinator.ts` — qualifies explicit `reviewPending` scheduler validation only with approved, integrated, commit-bound passing checks and no omissions.
- `tests/helpers/package.ts`, `tests/helpers/packaged-probe.ts` — isolated tarball consumers and installed runtime assertions.
- `tests/integration/15-package.test.ts`, `tests/smoke/package-install.test.ts`, `tests/e2e/full-workflow.test.ts` — bounded regression/smoke/demo entrypoints.
- `scripts/package-smoke.ts`, `.github/workflows/ci.yml` — portable demo and platform evidence artifacts.
- `README.md`, `docs/package-validation.md` — installation, reproducible demo and exact evidence/limitations.

## Related

- [Workflow controls and recovery](workflow-recovery.md)
- [Bounded review and repair](bounded-review-repairs.md)

## Notes
The production plugin now supplies visible chat workflow tools and automatic dual-review coordination. Deterministic package fixtures retain their explicit host inputs to exercise boundaries reproducibly. Native Linux/Windows and credentialed complete-workflow evidence are tracked separately. Long multi-worktree tests have explicit bounded deadlines; product timeouts are unchanged.

## Last updated

2026-10-15

The 2026-10-15 credentialed macOS trial reached verified dual review. The final package was installed outside the source checkout so clean builds cannot unload a running development-linked plugin. Native installed-package status and a real browser dashboard confirmed the same reviewed feature; see `docs/live-opencode-trial.md`. The source checkout remains unborn/uncommitted, so the native workflow used a committed temporary copy and retained its feature branch.

A normal OpenCode UI restart can reuse stale background-service location state after a plugin path update. Configuration inspection may show the new path while `plugin.list` still reports the old package. On 2026-10-15, refreshing the exact idle repository through authenticated `debug.location.evict` loaded the current package and restored all six slash commands; plain `opencode` autocomplete confirmed it. Global isolated-server tests alone do not prove an existing user service has reloaded. See `docs/troubleshooting.md`.

The foreground-setup upgrade exposed an old dashboard shutdown failure (`Server is not running`) during a configuration refresh. It left the repository host lease in `closing` with zero locations. The idle repository was recovered only after checking every job was terminal and no operation remained at intent, preserving the lease as an external backup, and clearing that exact abandoned shutdown record under its mutex. A subsequent authenticated location refresh loaded the new package and all six commands; the foreground setup tool was verified through native RPC. Avoid racing an explicit refresh with configuration-driven shutdown. Cleanup should eventually guarantee lease finalization even if an already-closed dashboard reports a stop error.
